  ok: Theorem A2: two-target unions are acyclic and Hamiltonian exactly as predicted for 3 <= n <= 60 (203 Hamiltonian pairs)
  ok: Theorem A3: over all 85261 triples with 3 <= n <= 24, the union is a Hamiltonian path iff (i) max degree <= 2, (ii) n-1 edges, (iii) gcd condition (758 paths)
  ok: Corollary A3''': all 11 primitive triples with t <= 60: the squares s^2, t^2, u^2 give a Hamiltonian path of [n] exactly at n = t^2-1, t^2 (and 17 for (3,4,5)) within [t^2-3, t^2+3], with the stated ends; (3,4,5) gives Q_15 with ends 9 and 8
  ok: Corollary A3'': three consecutive squares give a Hamiltonian union only at [(4, 15), (4, 16), (4, 17)] (j <= 25, n <= 700)
  ok: Lemma T: for 3 <= n <= 5000 every target in (max(P,Q), 2n-1] is 2P or 3Q
